[Unit]
Description=Hermes agent, WebUI and hindsight-client update with health assertion and rollback
Documentation=https://github.com/mnbf9rca/kubernetes_config/blob/master/docs/operations/hermes-vm-updates.md
# NO network-online.target ORDERING IS POSSIBLE HERE, and its absence is
# deliberate rather than an omission. This runs under the USER manager, and
# Debian ships no network-online.target for the user manager - confirmed by
# listing the directory. `Wants=` on a unit that cannot be loaded is tolerated
# in silence and `After=` on one imposes no ordering at all, so the two lines
# that used to sit here were dead, while reading to the next person as an
# ordering guarantee that never existed. They were removed on 2026-08-27,
# together with the identical pair on hermes-app-alive.service, because fixing
# one and leaving the other is worse than either state. DO NOT RE-ADD THEM.
#
# The cost is smaller here than on hermes-app-alive.service, and only because
# this unit has no timer: it is started on demand, on a machine that has been
# up for a while. Start it into a network that is not up yet and the run fails
# on its first fetch - but the healthchecks.io ping cannot leave either, so the
# report is silence rather than a red check. What absorbs that is the cadence:
# this check runs a ~45-day period with a 7-day grace, so one silent run was
# never going to be the alarm.

# THERE IS NO .timer UNIT FOR THIS SERVICE AND THERE MUST NOT BE ONE. `hermes
# update` sometimes carries manual steps - a config schema migration prompts,
# and a run that stashes local edits needs a human to look at what it stashed -
# so this is started on demand: by the operator, by /usr/local/bin/hermes-update,
# or by the 4-to-6-week update session. Its healthchecks.io check runs a ~45-day
# period with a 7-day grace to match that cadence. Arming a timer here would
# change the failure model, not just the schedule.
#
# hermes-app-alive.timer beside this file is NOT a counter-example: it drives a
# read-only heartbeat that mutates nothing and prompts for nothing. The rule is
# "updates are not scheduled", not "this directory holds no timers".

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=/home/hermes/bin/hermes-update.sh

# MUST MATCH $HERMES_USER_HOME IN hermes-update.sh, which pins the same path
# around the `import run_agent` health assertion. Python puts the current
# working directory on sys.path, so an import asserted from an unpinned
# directory is not the same assertion twice. Setting it here is not redundancy
# with the pin in the script: it makes the two AGREE BY CONSTRUCTION rather
# than by the coincidence that an unset WorkingDirectory= in a systemd USER
# unit happens to default to the user's home. Change one and change the other.
#
# For the record, so this is not "tidied away" on the strength of a survey
# note: the import does not actually depend on the working directory. The agent
# is installed in editable mode under PEP 660 and its import finder holds an
# ABSOLUTE path, so the module resolves from anywhere - verified on the VM from
# the filesystem root. The pin and this setting are belt and braces. Keep both.
WorkingDirectory=/home/hermes

# HERMES_HOME is the load-bearing one. `hermes update` installs dependencies
# with uv, and hermes resolves uv from $HERMES_HOME/bin/uv by ABSOLUTE PATH -
# managed_uv.py:53-63, whose module docstring says "no PATH probing, no conda
# guards, no multi-location resolution chains". Task 0 Step 10 confirmed there
# is no uv anywhere else on this VM: not on the ssh PATH, not on the user
# manager's PATH, not in ~/.local/bin. So a wrong-or-absent HERMES_HOME, not a
# short PATH, is what would make `hermes update` fail to find its own tooling.
# (An earlier draft, following the adversarial review's M12, put ~/.local/bin on
# PATH "so hermes update can find uv". That reasoning was wrong; the line below
# is kept only for git and the ordinary system tools.)
Environment=HERMES_HOME=/home/hermes/.hermes
Environment=PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin

# ONE value: the healthchecks.io ping UUID, which must never be carried in the
# repo. Mode 0600, written at install by `op read` piped over ssh. No leading
# '-': the unit must refuse to start when the file is absent, because a missing
# UUID would make a failed run indistinguishable from no run.
#
# The chat turn's credential is NOT here. The script reads the default profile's
# API_SERVER_KEY from ~/.hermes/.env at run time - the same file the gateway
# itself reads - so nothing is copied and there is one value to rotate, not two.
# An earlier draft carried HERMES_PROBE_API_KEY in this file for a probe profile
# that no longer exists; do not reinstate it.
EnvironmentFile=/home/hermes/.hermes/hermes-update.env

# DO NOT LOWER THIS. 10800 is a DERIVED number, not a round one.
#
# hermes-update.sh bounds every child that can block on a network, and the sum
# of those bounds along its longest route - the update succeeds, the health
# assertion fails, the full rollback runs, health is re-asserted - is 9040
# seconds, a little over 2h30m. Each figure in that sum is LIMIT + grace,
# because `timeout -k` sends SIGTERM at the limit and SIGKILL that much later
# again. It is the longest of the five routes through the script; the other
# four come to 8740, 8285, 7355 and 5945 seconds, and a run that simply
# succeeds is 5305. The script's own "the time budget" block carries the
# derivation.
#
# The remaining 1760 seconds are deliberate headroom, and they are headroom for
# a specific thing: 9040 bounds only the children the script wraps in
# `timeout`. The local git operations, the `sed` over the agent's environment
# file and the `python3` parse of the chat response sit outside that sum
# because none of them can block on a network, so none of them is bounded - but
# they are not free either.
#
# An earlier draft of this task said 3600. That would kill a legitimately long
# run part way through, quite possibly mid-rollback, which is the worst moment
# this system has. The point of the bound is that a WEDGED run fails rather
# than sitting in `activating` where nothing sees it - not to second-guess a
# run that is still working. On expiry systemd sends SIGTERM, which the script
# traps and converts to exit 143 so the EXIT trap still pings; without that
# trap a timeout would be pure silence.
#
# WHAT KEEPS TWO RUNS FROM OVERLAPPING IS THE SCRIPT'S OWN flock, NOT THIS UNIT.
# An earlier draft said systemd "already refuses a second concurrent start" and
# called that this unit's `concurrencyPolicy: Forbid`. It does not refuse: a
# `systemctl start` issued while a start job is in flight MERGES into that job
# and returns its result. The outcome is still right - systemd never runs a
# second ExecStart - but the mechanism matters twice over. First, the second
# caller is handed the FIRST run's exit status as though it were its own, which
# is a real triage trap for the entry point in ../bin/hermes-update. Second,
# systemd is not involved at all when the script is run directly over ssh, which
# the runbook allows; only the `flock` covers that. Read the analogy as being to
# the script, not to this file.
TimeoutStartSec=10800

# THE STOP BOUND EXISTS TO LET THE EXIT TRAP RUN, and it is long on purpose.
#
# In POSIX sh a trap cannot run while a foreground child is blocked; the shell
# runs it only once that child returns. hermes-update.sh sends its entire
# report - the healthchecks.io ping and its body - from an EXIT trap. So when
# systemd stops this unit, the SIGTERM is not acted on until the child the
# script is currently inside has finished. If TimeoutStopSec expired before
# then, systemd would follow with SIGKILL, which no shell can trap, and the run
# would report NOTHING - which is precisely the failure the traps exist to
# prevent.
#
# The longest single foreground child is `hermes update`, bounded at 1800
# seconds with a 300-second kill grace: 2100 seconds. After it returns the trap
# needs only seconds - a few `rm`s, the body, and one `curl` bounded at 15s
# (twice, worst case). 2400 gives the trap the full 2100-second wait plus 300
# seconds of margin, so a stop lands as a reported failure rather than silence.
#
# THE COST, stated plainly because it is real: `systemctl --user stop
# hermes-update` on a run that is inside `hermes update` can take up to 40
# minutes to return. Expect far less in practice - the default KillMode is
# `control-group`, so the stop's SIGTERM goes to EVERY process in the cgroup,
# the `timeout` wrapper and the update command included; the child normally dies
# at once and the trap then runs in seconds. The 40 minutes is the pathological
# case of a child that ignores SIGTERM for its whole limit, and it is that case
# the number has to cover. That is a long time to sit at a prompt, and it is the
# right trade. The alternative is a fast stop that SIGKILLs the shell during a
# forward-only config and state.db migration - the single worst interruption
# point in this system, per the --backup comment in the script's phase 1 - and
# reports nothing about it. An operator who genuinely needs the run gone now,
# and accepts both of those, can still say
# `systemctl --user kill -s SIGKILL hermes-update`.

TimeoutStopSec=2400

# No Restart=. A failed run must stay failed: the script has already rolled
# back and pinged, and re-running it automatically would re-attempt the same
# broken update against a freshly restored venv.
StandardOutput=journal
StandardError=journal

# No [Install] section. Nothing enables this unit; it is started by hand.
